{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall not bring the hire of a harlot</b>. The harlots would make offerings from their hire, thinking to obtain atonement for their sin, as our Rabbis mention (Leviticus Rabbah 3:1) in their parable: \"a vase among the apples and a share among the worthless.\" Therefore the Torah forbade the hire for any vow, for now they would thereby add sin upon sin. And the same is the matter of the price of a dog, because the hunters with dogs and the watchmen of the walls raise dogs that are fierce of spirit, harmful to the public, and they would vow from their price to be an atonement for themselves. And so the practice persists to this day among the huntsmen, the men of the hunt, that they set up the image of their dogs in covetousness before idolatry, that they may succeed through them. And the commentators said: because it is a way of disgrace. And He said (Deuteronomy 23:19):" "<b>Even both of them</b>, to include their derivatives, for example wheat and she made it into fine flour — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 23:19). But in the Gemara (Bava Kamma 65b) they said: Beit Shammai forbid and Beit Hillel permit; rather, \"they\" and not their offspring, \"they\" and not their derivatives. And the reason for <b>even both of them</b>: because the one in truth is a great abomination, and that is the harlot's hire, for through it the abomination of zimmah was committed, He said that HaShem will abhor even both of them. And like it (Proverbs 17:15): \"He who justifies the wicked and he who condemns the righteous — both of them alike are an abomination to HaShem\" — the lesser is suspended upon the greater. And likewise (Deuteronomy 22:22) <b>even both of them shall die</b>, as I have explained. Or the reason is that He already warned regarding disqualifications of the sacrifice in blemishes and said concerning them (Deuteronomy 17:1): \"You shall not sacrifice to HaShem your Elohim an ox or a sheep that has any blemish whatsoever, for that is an abomination to HaShem your Elohim\" — He added here to say that even the harlot's hire and the dog's price, whose body is unblemished, are also an abomination to HaShem your Elohim like them, and this is the correct interpretation. And likewise (Proverbs 20:12): \"The hearing ear and the seeing eye — HaShem has made even both of them\" — it alludes to man in its inclusiveness, saying that HaShem formed man and also made these within him. And likewise (Proverbs 20:10): \"Diverse weights and diverse measures — both of them alike are an abomination to HaShem\" — it alludes to the robber and the extortionist and every oppressor, for they are greater abominations than these, yet He does not mention them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The Aramaic parable fragment גייפא בתפוחיא ומפלגא לבאישיא is rendered literally; it appears to contrast something fine placed among apples with something assigned to the worthless."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:54:54.091736405Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}